<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="(3E)-4,8-dimethylnona-1,3,7-triene biosynthesis II" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6007" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; The two acyclic homoterpenes |FRAME:CPD-8844| (DMNT) and |FRAME:CPD-8846| (TMTT) are widespread constituents of flower fragrances |CITS:[boland89][gabler91],[donath95]|. These homoterpenes are released from the foliage of many plant species following herbivore damage |CITS:[boland92],[dicke94]|, and are part of the arsenal used in plant tritrophic &apos;indirect defense&apos;, which uses the volatile compounds to attract predators of the herbivores and help them locate their prey so that they do less damage to the host plants |CITS:[Dicke99][10517823][16723271]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: CPD-8844| is known to be produced from |FRAME: FARNESYL-PP| as described in the pathway |FRAME: PWY-5434|. However, an unexpected second route for its production has been discovered. In this route, whic occurs in roots, the compound is produced from the C30 triterpene diol |FRAME: CPD-10307| by the action of a cytochrome P450 monooxygenase encoded by the |FRAME: AT4G15330| gene |CITS: [25724638]|. The |FRAME: AT4G15330| gene clusters with the arabidiol synthase gene |FRAME: AT4G15340|, and both genes are coexpressed constitutively in the root stele and meristematic tissue |CITS: [25724638]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE" />
    <Author name="dreher" />
  </Authors>
